WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.712 --> 00:00:06.880
Now, Shannon had just finished
developing his theories related

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:08.890
to cryptography
and therefore was

00:00:08.890 --> 00:00:11.760
well aware that human
communication was

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:16.340
a mix of randomness and
statistical dependencies.

00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:18.980
Letters in our
messages were obviously

00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:21.550
dependent on previous
letters to some extent.

00:00:25.190 --> 00:00:28.650
And in 1949, he published
a groundbreaking paper,

00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:31.820
a mathematical theory
of communication.

00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:35.090
And in it, he uses Markov
models as the basis

00:00:35.090 --> 00:00:37.290
for how we can think
about communication.

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:39.460
And he starts with
a toy example.

00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:41.810
Imagine you encounter a
bunch of texts written

00:00:41.810 --> 00:00:44.500
in an alphabet of
A, B, and C. Perhaps

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:46.500
you know nothing
about this language,

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:51.220
but you notice As seem to clump
together while B and Cs do not.

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:53.110
He then shows that you
could design a machine

00:00:53.110 --> 00:00:57.750
to generate similar looking
text using a Markov chain.

00:00:57.750 --> 00:01:01.280
And he starts off with a
zeroth-order approximation,

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:03.370
which means we just
independently select

00:01:03.370 --> 00:01:06.864
each symbol, A, B, or C at
random, and form a sequence.

00:01:06.864 --> 00:01:08.280
However, notice
that this sequence

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:11.676
doesn't look like the original.

00:01:11.676 --> 00:01:13.300
He shows then you
could do a bit better

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:16.240
with a first-order approximation
where the letters are

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:18.550
chosen independently,
but according

00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:20.350
to the probability
of each letter

00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:22.120
in the original sequence.

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:25.370
So this is slightly better,
as As are now more likely,

00:01:25.370 --> 00:01:27.360
but it still doesn't
capture much structure.

00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:34.460
The next step is key.

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:36.250
A second-order
order approximation

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:40.270
takes into account each pair
of letters which can occur.

00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:42.650
And in this case, we
need three states.

00:01:42.650 --> 00:01:44.670
The first state
represents all pairs

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:48.200
which began with A, the second,
all pairs that begin with B,

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:51.230
and the third state, all
pairs that begin with C.

00:01:51.230 --> 00:01:55.040
And notice now that the A
cup as many AA pairs, which

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:58.040
makes sense since the
conditional probability of an A

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:02.150
after an A is higher in
our original message.

00:02:02.150 --> 00:02:05.180
Now we can generate a sequence
using this second-order model

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:06.620
easily, as follows.

00:02:06.620 --> 00:02:09.150
We start anywhere
and pick a tile,

00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:12.410
and we write down or
output the first letter

00:02:12.410 --> 00:02:15.710
and move to the cup defined
by the second letter.

00:02:15.710 --> 00:02:17.940
Then we pick a new tile
and repeat this process

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:18.481
indefinitely.

00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:22.800
Notice that this
sequence is starting

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:25.440
to look very similar to
the original message,

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:28.770
because this model is capturing
the conditional dependencies

00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:29.476
between letters.

00:02:32.716 --> 00:02:34.090
And if we want to
do even better,

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:36.250
we could move to a third
order approximation which

00:02:36.250 --> 00:02:40.000
takes into account groups of
three letters, or trigrams.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:42.020
And in this case, we
would need nine states.

00:02:45.050 --> 00:02:48.270
But next, Shannon applies
this exact same logic

00:02:48.270 --> 00:02:51.830
to actual English
texts, using statistics

00:02:51.830 --> 00:02:56.540
that were known for letters,
pairs, and trigrams, et cetera.

00:02:56.540 --> 00:02:58.100
And he shows the
same progression

00:02:58.100 --> 00:03:00.470
from zeroth-order,
random letters,

00:03:00.470 --> 00:03:06.600
to first-order, second-order,
and third-order sequences.

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:08.660
He then goes on and
tries this same thing

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:11.940
using words instead of letters.

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:15.860
And he writes "the resemblance
to ordinary English text

00:03:15.860 --> 00:03:19.510
increases quite
noticeably at each depth."

00:03:19.510 --> 00:03:22.990
Indeed, these machines were
producing meaningless text,

00:03:22.990 --> 00:03:24.620
though they contained
approximately

00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:28.085
the same statistical structure
you'd see in actual English.

00:03:32.010 --> 00:03:35.220
Shannon then proceeds to
define a quantitative measure

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:38.950
of information, as he realizes
that the amount of information

00:03:38.950 --> 00:03:43.530
in some message must be tied up
in the design of the machine,

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:47.300
which could be used to generate
similar looking sequences.

00:03:51.640 --> 00:03:54.960
Which brings us to his
concept of entropy.

